      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 1,
          "section": "Abstract",
          "quote": "In a previous work, the chemical elements necessary for the achievement of Niobium base superalloys were defined in order to get a structure equivalent to that of Nickel base superalloys, which contain ordered precipitates within a disordered solid-solution.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "40dfa7cd876bed5e:part:001"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "alloy_system_2",
      "name": "Nb-Hf-Ni alloy",
      "material_category": "refractory alloy",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "page": 1,
          "section": "Abstract",
          "quote": "Then, starting from the Nb-Hf-Ni ternary alloy thus defined, this paper demonstrates that the precipitation of an ordered Nb phase within a disordered Nb matrix can be achieved with lattice parameter coherency between the ordered precipitates and the disordered matrix.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "40dfa7cd876bed5e:part:001"
        },
        {
          "page": "13",
          "section": "5. Discussion",
          "quote": "The alloy, whose composition is 90.3 at% Nb, 5.8 at% Hf, and 3.9 at% Ni, shows a typical superalloy structure composed with a solid-solution (or matrix) containing ordered coherent precipitates.",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "40dfa7cd876bed5e:part:002"
        },
        {
          "page": "14",
          "section": null,
          "quote": "this Nb-Hf-Ni alloy was designed at the end of my post-doc",
          "table": null,
          "figure": null,
          "chunk_id": "40dfa7cd876bed5e:part:003"
        }
      ]
    }
